--TEST--
a parsed node is an instance of its own production's class, so the tree is typed per production rather than by a kind string alone — `rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert`, `rule:security/reflection-needs-no-capability`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert` asks for one type per production, and the
// class is where it is carried: `kind()` is the production's short name and the
// node's class is the production itself. Asserting them together is the point --
// a node that answered a plausible `kind()` from the wrong class fails here
// while looking right on either line alone.
var $tree = Core\Ast::parse("<?nvs echo 1 + 2;");
echo Core\Reflect::forObject($tree)->name(), "\n";
foreach ($tree->nodes() as Core\Ast\Node $node) {
    echo $node->kind(), " -> ", Core\Reflect::forObject($node)->name(), "\n";
}

// The same production is the same class every time it appears, and two parses
// of the same text agree -- the roster is the grammar's own table rather than
// something minted per tree.
var $again = Core\Ast::parse("<?nvs echo 1 + 2;");
bool $agreed = Core\Reflect::forObject($again)->name()
    == Core\Reflect::forObject($tree)->name();
echo "same class across two parses: ", $agreed ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// Neither half took a capability: parsing reads a string this program already
// holds and describing a value reads the class it already is
// (`rule:security/reflection-needs-no-capability`).
echo "declarations are their own productions\n";
var $decl = Core\Ast::parse("<?nvs class C { public function m(): void {} }");
foreach ($decl->nodes() as Core\Ast\Node $node) {
    echo "  ", Core\Reflect::forObject($node)->name(), "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Ast\File
Echo -> Core\Ast\Echo
Binary -> Core\Ast\Binary
Int -> Core\Ast\Int
Int -> Core\Ast\Int
same class across two parses: yes
declarations are their own productions
  Core\Ast\ClassDecl
  Core\Ast\Method
